WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:01.240
- [Narrator] Thanks to Dashlane

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:03.750
for keeping Legal Eagle in the air.

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:06.110
- So you don't work on
a contingency basis?

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:08.100
- No, money down.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:11.527
Oops shouldn't have this Bar
Association logo here either.

00:00:11.527 --> 00:00:13.500
(tearing)
(chewing)

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.637
- Yeah, he's just the best
worst lawyer of all time.

00:00:16.637 --> 00:00:19.304
(playful music)

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.750
Hey Legal Eagles, it's time
to think like a lawyer,

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:26.230
and today we are covering
one of my favorite episodes

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:29.430
of one of my favorite series of all time,

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:32.570
it's The Simpsons and it's
episode where Itchy and Scratchy

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:34.900
get taken to court for
copyright infringement.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.770
It's called The Day the Violence Died,

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:38.680
and while I will be reacting to it,

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.920
I think I could probably quote
this episode line for line,

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.150
it's really that good,
it's one of my favorites,

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:45.530
it involves Lionel Hutz.

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:47.200
- [Lionel] No, money down.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.610
- It's just, it's just, it's fantastic.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:52.460
So as always, be sure to comment
in the form of an objection

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:54.510
which I will either sustain or overrule,

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.270
and stick around until
the end of the episode

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:59.440
where I give The Simpsons,
The Day the Violence Died

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:01.610
a grade for legal realism.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:03.430
So without further ado, let's dig in

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:07.051
to The Simpsons, The
Day the Violence Died.

00:01:07.051 --> 00:01:10.270
(playful music)

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:12.290
So the background of this episode is that

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:15.350
it is the 75th anniversary
of Itchy &amp; Scratchy,

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:18.440
which is a clear stand-in for Disney,

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:22.305
a much more violent version of
the Walt Disney Corporation,

00:01:22.305 --> 00:01:25.808
and the town of Springfield
is throwing a parade

00:01:25.808 --> 00:01:28.840
for the creators of Itchy &amp; Scratchy,

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.190
which includes Roger Meyers Senior,

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:35.101
who is the guy who claims to
have invented Itchy &amp; Scratchy.

00:01:35.101 --> 00:01:37.902
The parade goes into Bumtown,

00:01:37.902 --> 00:01:42.050
where we find Bart about to meet a bum

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:45.538
who claims to have invented
Itchy &amp; Scratchy himself.

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:47.837
(upbeat music)

00:01:47.837 --> 00:01:49.087
- Hey, wait up!

00:01:50.312 --> 00:01:51.898
(tomatoes splattering)

00:01:51.898 --> 00:01:53.010
(blows raspberries)

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:55.740
- Get out of Bumtown, you no talent bum.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.050
- Bums.
- Show some respect, man,

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.310
that no talent created Itchy &amp; Scratchy.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:02.200
- He didn't create Itchy, I did.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:03.033
- Huh?

00:02:03.033 --> 00:02:05.850
- He stole the character from me in 1928.

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:08.370
When I complained, his thugs
kicked me out of his office

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:10.315
and dropped an anvil on me.

00:02:10.315 --> 00:02:11.570
(laughs)

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:14.829
- So this is interesting, this
is a normal copyright issue,

00:02:14.829 --> 00:02:17.160
great issues going on already.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:20.160
Here you have the theft
of intellectual property,

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.240
and purportedly, the theft of a character.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:26.132
Now a character as a part
from a normal literary or

00:02:26.132 --> 00:02:30.620
audiovisual work can actually
receive copyright protection.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:32.820
So for example we've seen
many times in litigation

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:36.970
that James Bond has copyright
protection as a character

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:40.580
apart from all of the 30
or 40 James Bond movies

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:42.490
that that character has appeared in.

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:46.110
So it is possible to quote
unquote steal a character

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:48.390
from a copyright
perspective, but remember,

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.530
you cannot steal ideas.

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:54.000
Ideas are not copyrightable,
it's only the expression itself

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.640
and I think that's going to be a big theme

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.120
throughout this episode.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:01.640
- You invented Itchy, the
Itchy &amp; Scratchy Itchy?

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:04.560
- Sure, in fact, I
invented the whole concept

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.410
of cartoon violence.

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:08.650
Before I came along,
all cartoon animals did

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:11.560
was play the ukulele, I changed all that.

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:13.673
- Well, I'm not calling you a liar but,

00:03:15.767 --> 00:03:17.902
but I can't think of a way
to finish that sentence.

00:03:17.902 --> 00:03:18.880
(laughs)

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:20.680
- Here we have another argument,

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:24.348
which is that he invented
the idea of cartoon violence.

00:03:24.348 --> 00:03:27.630
Now that almost certainly
he would not be able to have

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:29.710
copyright protection over.

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:33.320
The idea of cartoon
violence or the idea of

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:37.080
a romantic comedy or the
idea of a sci-fi space opera,

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.520
these are what we call scenes a faire,

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:41.330
which are not protectable.

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:44.710
They are stock scenes that
we see over and over and over

00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:46.200
again in many different industries

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.430
and different types of IP,

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:51.350
so he would not be able to have claim for

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:54.270
intellectual property over
the idea of cartoon violence,

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:56.930
even if cartoon characters were only

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:59.890
just playing the ukulele
before he came along.

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:02.870
- Itchy the Lucky Mouse
in Manhattan Madness.

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:05.330
- That's the first
Itchy cartoon ever made,

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:08.720
and it was made by me,
Chester J. Lampwick.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:11.483
Find me a 90-year-old projector
and I'll prove it to you.

00:04:11.483 --> 00:04:12.661
(playful music)

00:04:12.661 --> 00:04:14.311
- (laughs) They go to the school.

00:04:17.299 --> 00:04:21.095
(light piano music)

00:04:21.095 --> 00:04:23.610
Okay so right off the
bat, this film canister

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:28.406
contains the film of the original
Itchy &amp; Scratchy cartoon.

00:04:28.406 --> 00:04:31.170
The thing is, that is something that

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:33.490
lends itself to a valid copyright.

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:34.950
The thing about copyright is that

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:37.940
you can't have to petition
the government to get it.

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:40.110
There are reasons why you
would want to do that,

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:45.110
but as soon as you put your
expression into a tangible form

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:48.050
that's supposed to be
semi-permanent or permanent,

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:50.940
that is when you get the
copyright in the expression,

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.670
and this film reel is
going to be something

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:56.060
that could lend itself
to creating a copyright

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:58.807
in this film itself and in the character

00:04:58.807 --> 00:05:00.583
of Itchy and/or Scratchy.

00:05:02.239 --> 00:05:04.656
(kids laugh)

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:08.667
The concept of cartoon violence invented.

00:05:08.667 --> 00:05:10.853
- [Bart] All right Chester! (cheering)

00:05:10.853 --> 00:05:13.480
- [Millhouse] Way to go!

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:15.930
- Oh, so this is a great
little tidbit here.

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:19.972
It says copyright, that
little C in the circle, 1919.

00:05:19.972 --> 00:05:24.690
In 1919, you actually had
to put a copyright notice

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:26.430
on any work that you had.

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:28.530
If you didn't put a copyright notice

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:30.703
on your tangible expression,

00:05:30.703 --> 00:05:32.970
then you would not get a copyright.

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:35.600
So back in the day, it was
really, really important

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:37.940
to have that little symbol
to let everyone know

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:39.600
that this was a copyrighted work.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:42.530
So if Chester J. Lampwick
had not put that notice

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:45.300
on his film, he would not have a copyright

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:46.700
and he wouldn't have any claim

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:48.460
to the Itchy &amp; Scratchy fortune.

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:50.220
United States did away
with that requirement

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:53.570
in 1976 in the 1976 Copyright Act,

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:56.050
which really wasn't necessary at all.

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:58.510
It was a trap for the
unwary to not include

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:00.780
a copyright notice in
your actual expression,

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:03.020
so now you still get a copyright

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:05.240
regardless of whether you put the little C

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:08.330
with the date that says
that there's a copyright

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:09.810
on your particular work.

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:11.910
- That was Itchy all
right, you did invent him.

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:14.119
When people see this,
you'll be rich and famous!

00:06:14.119 --> 00:06:16.887
(tape crinkles)

00:06:16.887 --> 00:06:18.765
(laughs)

00:06:18.765 --> 00:06:20.277
(dramatic music)

00:06:20.277 --> 00:06:21.110
D'oh.

00:06:21.110 --> 00:06:22.400
- Yeah, as an evidentiary issue,

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:25.450
that makes it much, much
harder to prove your case.

00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:28.173
- Well, that brought
back a lot of memories.

00:06:29.046 --> 00:06:32.540
- I Can't Believe It's
a Law Firm. (laughs)

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:35.230
Okay, there are actual regulations

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:38.600
that prescribe what
lawyers are allowed to put

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:40.840
in terms of a name of their firms,

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:43.900
and that name absolutely
does not comply with that,

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:46.780
and it sort of implies that
it's not actually a law firm.

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:50.528
Although a query whether Lionel
Hutz is actually an attorney

00:06:50.528 --> 00:06:54.610
in The Simpsons, given
his run-ins with the Bar.

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:56.940
Oh, Lionel Hutz is my
favorite character ever,

00:06:56.940 --> 00:06:58.250
he's so good.

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:00.250
- All right gentlemen,
I'll take your case,

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:02.840
but I'm going to have to
ask for a $1,000 retainer.

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:04.337
- A thousand dollars?

00:07:04.337 --> 00:07:06.053
But your ad says no money down.

00:07:08.216 --> 00:07:10.861
- Oh, they got this all screwed up.

00:07:10.861 --> 00:07:13.821
(pen squeaking)

00:07:13.821 --> 00:07:15.330
(laughs)

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:17.670
- As I've covered already on this channel,

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:20.730
this is one of my favorite
lawyer jokes of all time,

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:24.140
and it so plays into Lionel Hutz as being

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:27.120
the most ridiculous,
terrible lawyer of all time.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:30.040
This is such a clear
violation of legal ethics,

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:32.670
where you can't put out an
ad like that to the public

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:34.030
and then completely redline it

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:35.570
right in front of your potential client

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:39.250
and claim that you only work
with a retainer and money down,

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:42.870
but the way that it works is
just so perfect, it's so good.

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:45.250
- So you don't work on
a contingency basis?

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:47.240
- No, money down.

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:50.776
Oops, shouldn't have this Bar
Association logo here either.

00:07:50.776 --> 00:07:52.630
(tearing)
(chewing)

00:07:52.630 --> 00:07:55.610
- Yeah, he's just the best
worst lawyer of all time.

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:58.518
- I thought I recognized you.

00:07:58.518 --> 00:08:02.450
I gave you a plate of
corn muffins back in 1947

00:08:02.450 --> 00:08:05.560
to paint my chicken coop,
and you never did it.

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:07.570
- Those corn muffins were lousy.

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:08.980
- Paint my chicken coop.

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:09.978
- Make me.

00:08:09.978 --> 00:08:11.200
(fists thudding)

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:14.210
- Yeah, that would be a
totally enforceable contract,

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:16.580
corn muffins for painting
the chicken coop,

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:19.820
and Chester J. Lampwick
would be in violation

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:22.750
of that contract for not
actually providing his labor.

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:24.870
I think the statute of
limitations has probably run

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:28.174
there now that it's 80
years in the future,

00:08:28.174 --> 00:08:31.800
but it would have been
an enforceable contract.

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:32.690
- Wait, wait, wait, wait, wait,

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:34.440
there's an easy way to get rid of Chester

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:36.824
without the guilt of sending
him back to the gutter.

00:08:36.824 --> 00:08:39.759
And all it will cost you
is a thousand dollars.

00:08:39.759 --> 00:08:42.407
(playful music)

00:08:42.407 --> 00:08:43.240
(laughs)

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:45.160
- Got legal fees from some family.

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:48.340
Okay, quick note, it
is possible to bankroll

00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:49.950
someone else's lawsuit.

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:53.130
Now traditionally, historically
that has been illegal,

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:54.800
it's been what's called champerty,

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:56.730
and that's been a
violation of legal ethics.

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:59.140
Those rules have largely been removed,

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:02.030
and it is possible to bankroll
someone else's lawsuit.

00:09:02.030 --> 00:09:04.230
This actually happened very recently

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:06.990
when Peter Thiel, the tech billionaire,

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:11.970
financed Hulk Hogan's lawsuit
against Gawker the website

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:15.250
for publishing Hulk Hogan's sex tape.

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:18.180
This is a part of a great
book by Ryan Holiday

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:21.250
called Conspiracy, that
talks about Peter Thiel

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:23.280
wanting to get revenge against Gawker,

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:25.960
so he bankrolled this
lawsuit that eventually

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:29.080
bankrupted the Gawker media enterprise.

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:31.720
So it is possible to do, it is legal,

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:33.110
might not be the nicest thing to do,

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:35.070
but you are allowed to do it.

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:37.720
(tense music)

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:41.490
- Exhibit A, Stable Itchy, dated 1928,

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:44.310
the very first Itchy &amp; Scratchy cartoon,

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:46.390
and the credits clearly state

00:09:46.390 --> 00:09:51.000
written, directed, and
created by Roger Myers.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:53.320
Music by Roger Myers and George Gershwin,

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:55.860
produced by Roger Myers
and Joseph P. Kennedy,

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:59.439
copyright 1928 by Roger Myers.

00:09:59.439 --> 00:10:01.720
- So we've already talked
about how important

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:04.050
that copyright notice was.

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:06.440
I don't know why they are jumping right to

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:08.570
closing argument here,
there would normally be

00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:12.210
years of discovery and motions on paper.

00:10:12.210 --> 00:10:13.770
This is the sort of thing
that would be dealt with

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:16.280
on summary judgment because
there is no evidence

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:18.100
on behalf of Chester J. Lampwick,

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:22.220
but as with most fictional
portrayals of legal proceedings,

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:23.950
they're just getting
right to the juicy parts.

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:26.480
- You will also notice Mr. Myers's name

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:28.890
and copyright notice on
the original drawings

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:31.310
of the other members of the
Itchy &amp; Scratchy family,

00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:33.630
Brown-Nose Bear, Disgruntled Goat,

00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:38.630
Flatulent Fox, Rich Uncle
Skeleton, and Dinner Dog.

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:42.200
- My client's film predates all
of those things, your Honor.

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:44.582
- You can't argue in the
middle of a statement.

00:10:44.582 --> 00:10:47.480
- Oh yes, I've forgotten,

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:51.430
your famous film, the one you
destroyed before the trial

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:53.950
and haven't been able to
able to find another copy of.

00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:55.960
Oh yes, that film.

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:58.581
- Yes, you don't have a copy, do you?

00:10:58.581 --> 00:11:00.360
(laughing)

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:03.070
- Now that sounds ridiculous,
and it is ridiculous

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:04.260
in the middle of trial like that,

00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:08.118
but in real life, Lionel Hutz
would have been able to ask

00:11:08.118 --> 00:11:11.142
if the owners of Itchy &amp; Scratchy

00:11:11.142 --> 00:11:14.950
have old copies of the 1919 film

00:11:14.950 --> 00:11:17.090
that is the basis for his claim.

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:20.580
And if they did, they would be
obligated to turn that over.

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:22.420
Now you might think that's crazy,

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:26.170
why would you turn over negative
evidence against yourself?

00:11:26.170 --> 00:11:29.190
Well the rules of civil
discovery require you

00:11:29.190 --> 00:11:33.060
to turn it over because we
don't want trial by ambush,

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:35.470
we want everyone to put all
of their cards on the table,

00:11:35.470 --> 00:11:37.420
and if there's evidence that you have

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:39.550
that's bad for your case, you have a duty

00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:41.200
to turn that over to the other side

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:43.940
if you get to that part
of the discovery process.

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:47.500
So Lionel Hutz, you know,
he's a terrible lawyer,

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:50.344
but even a blind squirrel
finds a nut sometimes.

00:11:50.344 --> 00:11:51.660
- [Nelson] Ha ha!

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:53.350
- So uh, good job for him.

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:55.760
- Roger Myers didn't create
any of his characters,

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:56.997
he stole them all.

00:11:56.997 --> 00:11:59.800
(dramatic music)
(crowd murmurs)

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:01.960
The only characters Myers
could ever come up with

00:12:01.960 --> 00:12:04.180
were pathetic stick figures with the words

00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:07.780
Sarcastic Horse and Manic
Mailman printed on them.

00:12:07.780 --> 00:12:08.970
And they stank.

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:12.100
- So who is the voice
of Chester J. Lampwick?

00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:13.540
That sounds really, hold on a second,

00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:14.473
I need to look this up because

00:12:14.473 --> 00:12:16.307
that guy sounds really familiar.

00:12:16.307 --> 00:12:17.482
(dinging)

00:12:17.482 --> 00:12:19.550
(gasps) It's Kirk Douglas.

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:21.210
Oh, I didn't know that, that's fantastic.

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:23.700
- Mr. Hutz, we've been
in here for four hours.

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:25.890
Do you have any evidence at all?

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:27.930
- Well your Honor, we've
got plenty of hearsay

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:31.079
and conjecture, those
are kinds of evidence.

00:12:31.079 --> 00:12:32.740
(laughs)

00:12:32.740 --> 00:12:34.870
That's actually true,
hearsay and conjecture

00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:37.000
are kinds of evidence.

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:39.650
They're not good forms of evidence,

00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:42.300
but technically they
are types of evidence.

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:44.890
Many of the lawyers that
I know use that line

00:12:44.890 --> 00:12:48.520
all the time, it's a staple
among lawyers, it's fantastic.

00:12:48.520 --> 00:12:49.600
(dramatic music)

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:52.400
- I knew I had seen this
exact scene somewhere else.

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:54.960
It was in the movie
Mr. Lampwick showed me.

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:58.640
Ladies and gentlemen, this
drawing was made in 1919,

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:01.492
nine years before Roger
Myers made his first

00:13:01.492 --> 00:13:03.273
Itchy &amp; Scratchy cartoon.

00:13:03.273 --> 00:13:07.250
- Hmm, yeah, but how do we
know that that's authentic?

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:08.650
There's a whole authentication process

00:13:08.650 --> 00:13:10.860
that you have to go through to prove that

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:13.350
the evidence that you are putting forward

00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:14.850
is what it purports to be.

00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:16.970
I don't think this evidence

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:18.190
would be admissible at this point.

00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:22.053
- Okay, maybe my dad did
steal Itchy, but so what?

00:13:22.053 --> 00:13:24.695
Animation is built on plagiarism.

00:13:24.695 --> 00:13:28.030
If it weren't for someone
plagiarizing the Honeymooners,

00:13:28.030 --> 00:13:29.910
we wouldn't have the Flintstones.

00:13:29.910 --> 00:13:32.280
If someone hadn't ripped
off Sergeant Bilco,

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:33.790
there'd be no Tomcat.

00:13:33.790 --> 00:13:36.270
Huckleberry Hound,
Chief Wiggum, Yogi Bear,

00:13:36.270 --> 00:13:38.420
ha, Andy Griffith.

00:13:38.420 --> 00:13:41.904
- One of the all-time great
side gags of the Simpsons

00:13:41.904 --> 00:13:45.110
is to break the fourth
wall and say that the idea

00:13:45.110 --> 00:13:48.840
of Chief Wiggum is stolen
from somewhere else.

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:50.960
Just, just A+,

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:52.660
and there's some truth
to what he's saying,

00:13:52.660 --> 00:13:55.730
that these characters are often homages

00:13:55.730 --> 00:14:00.391
and frankly, stolen from other places.

00:14:00.391 --> 00:14:04.360
So whether you get copyright protection

00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:07.960
over a specific character
itself is up for grabs,

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:12.650
and not all taking is illegal,
not all copying is illegal.

00:14:12.650 --> 00:14:14.404
- Well Itchy &amp; Scratchy are gone,

00:14:14.404 --> 00:14:18.298
but here's a cartoon that
tries to make learning fun.

00:14:18.298 --> 00:14:19.624
(giggles)

00:14:19.624 --> 00:14:21.050
(groans)

00:14:21.050 --> 00:14:22.090
Sorry about this, kids.

00:14:22.090 --> 00:14:22.923
- Oh I know what this is.

00:14:22.923 --> 00:14:25.430
This is one of the most
brilliantly-written parodies

00:14:25.430 --> 00:14:27.700
of all time, I can sing this entire song.

00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:31.910
- Hey, who left all this garbage
on the steps of Congress?

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:33.514
- I'm not garbage.

00:14:33.514 --> 00:14:35.587
♪ I'm an amendment to be ♪

00:14:35.587 --> 00:14:37.685
♪ Yes an amendment to be ♪

00:14:37.685 --> 00:14:41.549
♪ And I'm hoping that they'll ratify me ♪

00:14:41.549 --> 00:14:44.066
♪ There's a lot of flag burners ♪

00:14:44.066 --> 00:14:46.333
♪ Who have got too much freedom ♪

00:14:46.333 --> 00:14:47.580
♪ I want to make it ♪

00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:48.936
- First Amendment violation.

00:14:48.936 --> 00:14:52.013
♪ Make it legal for
policemen to beat them ♪

00:14:52.013 --> 00:14:55.086
♪ Because there's limits
to our liberties ♪

00:14:55.086 --> 00:14:58.202
♪ At least I hope and
pray that there are ♪

00:14:58.202 --> 00:15:02.725
♪ Because those liberal
freaks go too far ♪

00:15:02.725 --> 00:15:04.030
(laughing)

00:15:04.030 --> 00:15:06.323
- Dad, can we have $183,000?

00:15:07.570 --> 00:15:08.610
- What for?

00:15:08.610 --> 00:15:11.559
- Lisa and I want to finance
a series of animated cartoons.

00:15:11.559 --> 00:15:12.853
- Oh, forget it.

00:15:14.310 --> 00:15:15.520
- Animation Legal Precedents.

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:17.760
Copyright Law 1918 to 1923.

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:19.890
That is such a great little detail there

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:21.900
because there actually was an amendment

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:26.290
to the Copyright Act in 1923
that changed copyright law,

00:15:26.290 --> 00:15:29.600
and in fact, because of the
change in copyright law,

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:33.080
all works before 1923
are out of copyright,

00:15:33.080 --> 00:15:36.789
so ironically although the
litigation over this case

00:15:36.789 --> 00:15:41.023
of a copyright from 1919
is raging in this episode,

00:15:41.023 --> 00:15:45.591
now looking back, all of this
would be out of copyright.

00:15:45.591 --> 00:15:48.400
The copyright in Itchy &amp; Scratchy would be

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:50.500
null and void and all of that stuff

00:15:50.500 --> 00:15:52.490
would have entered into the public domain.

00:15:52.490 --> 00:15:54.760
So it's a great little detail
that they are looking at

00:15:54.760 --> 00:15:57.550
the correct copyright law for the time,

00:15:57.550 --> 00:16:00.550
and since the work was created in 1919,

00:16:00.550 --> 00:16:03.390
you'd have to look at the law that existed

00:16:03.390 --> 00:16:05.980
before the Copyright Act of 1923,

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:09.440
because that's how you sort of
retroactively apply the law.

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:11.378
Just, just from top to bottom,

00:16:11.378 --> 00:16:13.990
one of the best Simpsons
episodes of all time.

00:16:13.990 --> 00:16:16.120
- When no one could think of a plan

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:19.470
to resurrect Itchy &amp;
Scratchy, a young boy,

00:16:19.470 --> 00:16:22.250
a wonderful, irrepressible young boy

00:16:22.250 --> 00:16:25.135
took it on his own to solve the problem.

00:16:25.135 --> 00:16:29.300
He discovered that the
postal service's Mr. Zip

00:16:29.300 --> 00:16:31.730
was just a rip-off of my father's

00:16:31.730 --> 00:16:34.637
stick figure character Manic Mailman.

00:16:34.637 --> 00:16:36.180
(laughs)

00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:40.540
So the government gave me
a huge cash settlement.

00:16:40.540 --> 00:16:42.570
- So that sounds totally ridiculous

00:16:42.570 --> 00:16:44.330
and there are still the
questions of whether you can

00:16:44.330 --> 00:16:46.840
even have a copyright in
a not really thought-out

00:16:46.840 --> 00:16:49.510
character like that, but
a situation almost exactly

00:16:49.510 --> 00:16:52.040
like that actually did
happen in a famous case

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:53.760
involving the US Postal Service.

00:16:53.760 --> 00:16:55.510
The US Postal Service created a stamp

00:16:55.510 --> 00:16:58.610
where they took a photo
of the Korean War Memorial

00:16:58.610 --> 00:17:02.210
in Washington DC, which
is a series of statues

00:17:02.210 --> 00:17:05.600
with soldiers who were
wading through the snow,

00:17:05.600 --> 00:17:06.730
and they put it on a stamp.

00:17:06.730 --> 00:17:10.110
The thing is, the artist
who created the statues

00:17:10.110 --> 00:17:13.620
still had a copyright in the statues.

00:17:13.620 --> 00:17:14.577
You can have a copyright in statues

00:17:14.577 --> 00:17:19.577
just look you can have a
copyright in paintings or a novel,

00:17:19.939 --> 00:17:23.180
so when they created this
stamp without his permission,

00:17:23.180 --> 00:17:25.090
they committed copyright infringement

00:17:25.090 --> 00:17:29.160
in the same way that the US
Postal Service supposedly

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:32.550
created copyright infringement
in this particular case.

00:17:32.550 --> 00:17:35.350
So things like that actually do happen

00:17:35.350 --> 00:17:38.210
and you can sometimes get
a big cash settlement from

00:17:38.210 --> 00:17:41.970
the government when they
involve copyright infringement.

00:17:41.970 --> 00:17:45.976
- And Itchy &amp; Scratchy
Studios is back in business.

00:17:45.976 --> 00:17:47.260
(triumphant music)
(electricity buzzing)

00:17:47.260 --> 00:17:49.250
Thanks to you, Lester.

00:17:51.668 --> 00:17:53.050
(gasping)

00:17:53.050 --> 00:17:55.630
- What the hell is going on?

00:17:55.630 --> 00:17:58.390
- And they end the episode
poking fun at the fact

00:17:58.390 --> 00:18:00.380
that their own characters are derivative,

00:18:00.380 --> 00:18:03.287
just so good.

00:18:03.287 --> 00:18:05.954
(playful music)

00:18:06.980 --> 00:18:09.890
But now it's time to give
this episode of the Simpsons

00:18:09.890 --> 00:18:11.744
a grade for legal realism.

00:18:11.744 --> 00:18:13.810
(gavel bangs)

00:18:13.810 --> 00:18:16.160
So on the one hand, you
have a brilliant story

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:19.120
involving some really detailed aspects

00:18:19.120 --> 00:18:21.900
of copyright law that you
really don't see all that often,

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:23.760
and for the most part it
gets the copyright stuff

00:18:23.760 --> 00:18:27.155
exactly right, right down
to the dates involved

00:18:27.155 --> 00:18:29.120
where the copyright law changed.

00:18:29.120 --> 00:18:32.100
You have probably one
of the best depictions

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:34.890
of a Schoolhouse Rock
parody that is not only,

00:18:34.890 --> 00:18:38.400
I think hilarious but exactly
right on First Amendment law.

00:18:38.400 --> 00:18:40.630
But on the other hand,
you have a trial that is

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:43.720
abbreviated to be the
shortest trial known to man,

00:18:43.720 --> 00:18:45.850
without any of the boring parts,

00:18:45.850 --> 00:18:48.050
and the worst lawyer known to man

00:18:48.050 --> 00:18:49.710
in the form of Lionel Hutz,

00:18:49.710 --> 00:18:51.070
but in some sense, the writers must know

00:18:51.070 --> 00:18:52.940
what they're doing because
there's no way to write

00:18:52.940 --> 00:18:56.560
a lawyer that bad without
knowing what a lawyer should do.

00:18:56.560 --> 00:18:58.680
So all in all, I will give this episode

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:00.690
of the Simpsons an A-.

00:19:00.690 --> 00:19:02.610
Maybe I'm giving them a
high grade just because

00:19:02.610 --> 00:19:04.716
I love this episode so, so much,

00:19:04.716 --> 00:19:07.390
but it's my show and I
can do whatever I want.

00:19:07.390 --> 00:19:09.380
- There's something unsettling about that.

